WEBVTT

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:08.430
Leave it to Beaver, starring Barbara Billingsley,

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:27.660
Hugh Beaumont, Tony Dow, Jerry Mathers as the Beaver.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:43.300
What's in the package?

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:45.140
Oh, I picked up two or three books.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:47.380
I've been sort of neglecting my reading lately.

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:50.160
Well, I guess that's as good an excuse as any

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.300
for not talking to me.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:54.950
Silly, dear, I don't need an excuse to not talk to you.

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:57.670
Why'd you buy two or three books?

00:00:58.130 --> 00:00:59.930
Well, it just happens that I thought I might like

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.420
to try and improve my mind.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:05.370
Oh, and you couldn't possibly improve your mind

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:06.190
by talking to me.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.500
All right, dear, we'll forget all about improving our minds.

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:13.910
For the next few evenings, we'll just sit around together

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:15.890
like a couple of morons and watch television.

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:17.320
That's better.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.440
Hey, I've got some good news for Wally.

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:23.390
I was able to change my appointment for Friday afternoon,

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:25.530
so we're going to be able to drive up to Linwood

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:26.610
for the track meet after all.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:27.790
Oh, word, that's wonderful.

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:29.970
Wally's asked about it ever since he got home from school.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.720
Well, I better go up and tell him.

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:35.260
Dear, maybe we could stop and eat on the way home.

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:38.720
That sounds like propaganda to get out of cooking dinner.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:40.660
That's exactly what it is.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:55.020
Hey, Beaver, what are you doing?

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:56.400
Thinking.

00:01:57.770 --> 00:01:58.330
Thinking what?

00:01:59.380 --> 00:01:59.980
Thinking what?

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.200
should do my homework now or after supper well how long have you been thinking that i don't know

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:12.380
guess about a half hour well heck in that time you could have had most of your homework done by now

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:25.440
she that's right i was so busy thinking i didn't think of that hi dad beaver's goofing off i am

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.710
You want something, Dad?

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:29.970
Yeah, it looks like we're all...

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.220
We're all going to be able to drive up to Linwood to that track meet Friday afternoon.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.180
Gee, Dad, I thought you had some kind of a meeting in the office.

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:37.500
I postponed it.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:41.200
And your mother's not going to the luncheon, and we're going to pick Beaver up after school.

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:45.820
Boy, I guess if I goof up now, you'd just have to leave me up there, huh, Dad?

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.510
Well, you've been doing pretty well in practice, haven't you?

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:54.870
Well, I'm okay in the 100, but I'm still tying up a little at the end of the 220.

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:57.870
At least he doesn't get sick behind the grandstands anymore.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:02:59.900
Now, cut it out.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:02.520
Well, I'm sure you're going to do just fine, Wally.

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:07.480
Beaver, one of these days you might turn into a runner.

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:11.270
I don't think so, Dad. I don't like getting sick.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:21.850
After school, Richard?

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:24.380
Anything that's after school.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:27.130
Yeah, I guess I do, too.

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:29.830
Hey, you want to mess around tomorrow and the next afternoon?

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.010
I mess around tomorrow.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.120
The day after that, I have to go up to Linwood

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:35.620
and watch my brother run and attract me.

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:39.160
Gee, Beaver, you mean you really like your own brother?

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:42.670
He's not so bad. Don't you like your brother, Richard?

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:46.500
No, I like him when he's not hittin' me.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.920
The only trouble is, he's all the time hittin' me.

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:52.060
How come he hit you?

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:56.450
I don't know. Guess cuz I'm his brother.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:57.810
Yeah.

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:03.900
Hey, is Wally going to win a whole bunch of medals at the track meet?

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:05.740
They don't give medals.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:07.410
Well, then what do you get for winning?

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:12.000
You get your name in the paper, and a lot of girls hang around you.

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:14.560
I'd rather have the medals.

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:18.040
Yeah, I guess Wally would, too.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:19.740
But you know how it works.

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:20.560
Yeah.

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:25.130
Well, how you doing, boys?

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:26.720
Fine, Mom.

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:28.090
Hi, Mrs. Kleber.

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:29.870
Thanks a lot for the cookies and junk.

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:30.910
You're welcome.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:34.820
Beaver, you got home before Wally today.

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:36.370
Sure, Mom.

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:38.450
He's got track practice to warm up.

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:41.170
They don't practice real hard just before a big meet.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:42.600
They just wanna heal up their muscles.

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:46.450
Well, Richard, when you get to high school,

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:47.430
are you gonna be a runner?

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:50.660
No, I think I'll be a bicycle racer.

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:52.610
Better at sitting than I am at running.

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:08.940
Hey, Wally, you're down for the 100, the 220, and the relay.

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:10.810
Yeah, how about that?

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:13.100
I fixed it for you with the coach, Wally.

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:15.730
Yeah, sure, Eddie.

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:17.830
Hey, Lumpy, look at that.

00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:19.830
You finally made the traveling squad.

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:21.750
Your third man on the shot put.

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:24.070
Where'd you pop to, bribe the coach?

00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:26.140
What do you mean?

00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:28.190
Better practice ought to have been number one.

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:29.810
Except I've been too busy studying.

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:31.650
Well, sure.

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:35.060
Anyway, as manager, I told the coach to give you a break.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:37.340
Gee, thanks a lot, Eddie.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:39.300
You really got a drag with the coach.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:40.220
Ah, yeah.

00:05:40.750 --> 00:05:42.290
Poor guy needs someone to lean on.

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:44.330
Like I told him yesterday, I said, Harry,

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:46.090
uh, good afternoon, Mr. Henderson.

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:49.440
Uh, we were just discussing our lineup for the meat Friday.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:53.810
I believe that we'll translate it easily, Mr. Henderson, sir.

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:55.830
Thank you, Haskell.

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:58.040
You have no idea how much that relieves my mind.

00:05:58.470 --> 00:05:59.980
I'm going over to the office for a few.

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:02.920
Would you mind taking this basket of wet towels

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:04.740
down to the laundry room as I asked you before?

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:06.880
Yes, sir, Mr. Henderson. Thank you, sir.

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:09.960
Cleaver, I want you fellas to get plenty of sleep

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:12.290
the next couple of nights, and be on your toes Friday.

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:14.170
Oh, sure thing, Mr. Henderson.

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:16.920
If you'd like, Coach, I'll phone them up tonight

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:18.260
and remind them to go to bed early.

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:21.030
I don't think that'll be necessary, Haskell.

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:21.750
Yes, sir.

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:24.950
Another thing, on this trip, if there's any horsing around on the bus

00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:27.190
like there was last time, the guy who starts it

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:28.710
is off the squad. Understand?

00:06:29.390 --> 00:06:29.910
Yes, sir.

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:30.460
Yes, sir.

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:30.980
Yes, sir.

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:36.290
Haskell, the towels?

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:37.350
Yes, sir.

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:38.330
I'm the double, Mr. Henderson.

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:40.240
See you later, fellas.

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:50.030
Hey, Eddie, why don't you lay off that baloney?

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:51.250
You're always giving the coach.

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:53.050
He doesn't fall for that stuff.

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:55.950
Yeah, you're just jealous because I'm in good with him.

00:06:56.330 --> 00:06:57.190
Isn't he lumpy?

00:06:57.490 --> 00:06:58.130
That's right, Eddie.

00:06:58.340 --> 00:06:59.940
It's just a case of sour apple.

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:02.790
Sour grapes, you dope.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:05.840
Oh, yeah, grapes.

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:08.520
Why don't you guys grow up?

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:10.330
Give me the towel.

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:25.070
Whoa.

00:07:25.730 --> 00:07:29.000
Hey, f*** out, Eddie.

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:29.980
Coming up, don't...

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:30.940
Look at me, Sam.

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:35.660
I'll fix you, you wise guy.

00:07:39.820 --> 00:07:40.720
Hey, pal.

00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:48.590
Cleaver?

00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:50.870
Uh, yes, sir?

00:07:53.510 --> 00:07:55.430
Didn't I just make a speech about horsing around?

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:55.890
Wally?

00:07:56.770 --> 00:07:57.930
Oh, hello, Mr. Henderson.

00:07:59.090 --> 00:07:59.490
Rutherford.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:06.400
what are you up to gee nothing i was just gonna take my shower all right cleaver who are you

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:20.220
throwing towels at nobody i guess oh just having a gay time by yourself huh well kind of all right

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:29.680
cleaver nobody's too important for this team so you're off it for a week gee you mean it you mean

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:29.960
i don't

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.050
get to run at Linwood? That's right.

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:34.680
We're off for one full week. And you can just think

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:36.620
about that next time you feel like throwing towels.

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:41.650
Yes, sir. Gee, Wally,

00:08:41.810 --> 00:08:45.160
that's a tough break. Look, Lumpy, why didn't you

00:08:45.160 --> 00:08:47.420
say something? It was you and that wise guy

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:49.660
Eddie that started it. Well, I would've,

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:52.490
but my pop was driving up

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:54.540
to Linwood to watch me tomorrow, and I just

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:55.800
couldn't disappoint Daddy.

00:08:57.080 --> 00:08:58.320
Ah, sure you couldn't.

00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:05.060
How many times have I got to tell you guys not to leave your towels laying around here?

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:06.680
Let's get with it, man.

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:24.840
When we go to Linwood on Friday, can we take along a whole bunch of junk to eat while he wants to track me?

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:28.570
Well, Beaver, sounds like you're more interested in eating than you are in watching the meat.

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:29.840
Sure, Dad.

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:31.980
Well, that's half the fun of sports,

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:33.960
sitting there and stuffing yourself while you watch them.

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:37.980
Well, Beaver, I guess I can pick some sandwiches to take along.

00:09:38.540 --> 00:09:40.700
Dear, what time are we going to start?

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:42.980
Well, I thought I'd leave the office about 2.30.

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:45.220
I'll pick you up here, and we'll get Beaver at school.

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:47.800
We don't want to miss seeing our star athlete

00:09:47.800 --> 00:09:49.900
run rings around those Linwood boys, do we, Wally?

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:52.290
Uh, yeah, sure, Dad.

00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:56.780
But, um, there's something I want to tell you and Mom about the track meet.

00:09:57.040 --> 00:09:58.940
We know, Wally. You've told us before.

00:09:59.290 --> 00:09:59.970
I promise...

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:05.040
I won't embarrass you by telling people in the stands that you're my son and your mother won't stand up and squeal

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:10.200
Come on Wally when you're running. Sure, Wally. We won't even let anybody know you belong to us

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:20.480
And then on the way home we can all stop by the colonial house and have dinner. Well, I don't know if we can do all that

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:29.310
Well, you mean you want to ride home with the team or something? Well, I don't think I can do that either because

00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:31.720
I'm not riding up there with him.

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:34.650
Wally, is there some trouble about this meet?

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:41.200
Well, kind of. I am. I got kicked off the team.

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:42.960
You got kicked off the team?

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:45.740
What did you do? Kill the coach or something?

00:10:47.260 --> 00:10:48.640
Yes, Wally. What did you do?

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:53.760
Well, some of the guys were throwing wet towels in the locker room,

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:56.950
and the coach came in, and he threw me off the team for a week.

00:10:58.510 --> 00:10:59.870
Well, were you the only one throwing?

00:11:01.190 --> 00:11:03.250
No, but I was the only one that got caught.

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:06.140
Oh, Wally, this is such a disappointment.

00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:07.440
Why, we had the whole day planned.

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:10.320
Yeah, I know, Mom.

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:12.160
Well, it's Wally's own fault.

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:13.880
The coach has to have discipline,

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:16.360
and the boys can't go around breaking the rules.

00:11:17.050 --> 00:11:19.580
Ward, couldn't we? I'll get it.

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:21.170
No, I'll get it.

00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:24.220
Wally, I'm ashamed of you.

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:26.430
Yeah, I'm ashamed of him too, Mom.

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:36.960
Hello. Oh, hello, Fred. It's, uh, Fred Rutherford. Yes, Fred?

00:11:37.340 --> 00:11:40.210
Oh, Ward. I was just talking to my lad Clarence.

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:44.100
He tells me your boy was dropped off the track team today.

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:48.140
Well, that was certainly nice of Lumpy to tell you that, Fred.

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:50.040
Tough break, old man.

00:11:50.650 --> 00:11:54.930
Gwen and I had thought we could get together for dinner after the meet and have a little fun fest.

00:11:57.480 --> 00:11:59.960
Yes, well, I'm afraid the little, uh, fun...

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:01.960
We'll have to come some other time, Fred.

00:12:02.430 --> 00:12:03.710
Well, I guess it's up to Clarence

00:12:03.710 --> 00:12:05.570
to uphold the honor of Mayfield High.

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:13.450
Yes, Fred. Well, thank you. Goodbye.

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:23.260
Fred Want?

00:12:24.950 --> 00:12:27.330
He just wanted to tell me that it's now up to Lumpy

00:12:27.330 --> 00:12:29.070
to uphold the honor of Mayfield High.

00:12:33.450 --> 00:12:35.850
Jim, I'm sorry, Dad.

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:41.220
That's all right. Sit up straight, Beaver.

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:44.820
Gee, Dad, don't yell at me. I didn't draw any towels.

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:07.460
Honey, I've been thinking, why don't you just call a coach up and ask him to put Wally back on the team?

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:10.560
Well, June, you just don't do things like that.

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:17.290
Well, couldn't you call him and ask him to put Wally off the team some other week when we haven't made a lot of plans?

00:13:19.170 --> 00:13:23.500
No, dear. I'm sorry. I'm just not a grandstand father.

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:28.720
Well, you could at least call a coach and tell him what a nice boy Wally is.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:36.530
june obviously from the coach's point of view nice boys just don't throw wet towels and anyway

00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:40.530
even if it would work i don't want wally getting the idea that every time he does something wrong

00:13:40.530 --> 00:13:46.760
i'm going to come along straighten things out for him well i don't believe that wally did it oh june

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:52.810
come on let's face it dear our boy just isn't a little angel why of course he is

00:13:52.810 --> 00:13:56.150
You ought to see how sweet he looks when he's asleep.

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:06.980
Tell me something. In the locker room, did you all of a sudden just start going flaky and throwing the towels?

00:14:07.340 --> 00:14:13.870
No, I didn't just start throwing towels. First Eddie threw one at me, and then Lumpy threw one at me.

00:14:14.420 --> 00:14:16.380
And I was chasing him when the coach walked in.

00:14:17.540 --> 00:14:28.080
Yeah, all that happens. Once Whitey pushed me into Larry, and I got sent to Mrs. Rayburn's office for fighting.

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:31.800
How come you didn't squeal on Lumpy and Eddie?

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:35.240
Well, how come you didn't squeal on Whitey and Larry?

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:37.130
No, that's right.

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:39.500
I wouldn't have squealed on them,

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:42.360
even if Mrs. Rayburn would have brought the janitor in

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:44.260
and he would have twisted my arm off.

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:46.320
Yeah, sure.

00:14:48.150 --> 00:14:51.110
I guess you feel pretty bad about getting bounced off the team, huh, Wally?

00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:54.200
Yeah, I'll say I feel bad.

00:14:58.230 --> 00:14:59.970
Maybe ten minutes before the...

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:03.620
bus leaves for Linwood, Eddie and Lumpy will come running up and they'll say,

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:07.810
Coach, we couldn't keep quiet any longer. We're the ones who started the towel

00:15:07.810 --> 00:15:11.510
fight and then the coach will put you on the bus and then the last second you

00:15:11.510 --> 00:15:17.920
won the whole track meet. Beaver, where do you get that kind of jazz? On television.

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:22.920
It happens every week on Men of Annapolis. Look, it's not gonna happen here in

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:24.420
Mayfield, so forget it, huh?

00:15:40.770 --> 00:15:43.090
Yeah, they were having a pep rally at school.

00:15:43.970 --> 00:15:45.870
I didn't feel so peppy, so I came home.

00:15:47.620 --> 00:15:52.470
Wally, do you think it would help if I called and talked to the coach?

00:15:53.320 --> 00:15:56.200
Oh, gee, Mom, don't do that. He's sore enough at me already.

00:15:57.000 --> 00:15:59.240
Well, he wouldn't be if he could just see you asleep.

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:06.400
Oh, nothing. Did you see Beaver on the way home?

00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:10.220
Well, about 3 o'clock, I saw him and Richard over in the park.

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:12.040
They were chasing a girl.

00:16:13.310 --> 00:16:14.130
Chasing a girl?

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:17.380
Well, yeah, she had Richard's lunchbox,

00:16:17.540 --> 00:16:18.920
and they were trying to get it back from her.

00:16:20.330 --> 00:16:22.270
Gee, Wally, that's an hour ago.

00:16:22.390 --> 00:16:23.330
I wonder why he isn't home.

00:16:23.550 --> 00:16:25.660
Well, she was a pretty good runner.

00:16:25.820 --> 00:16:26.900
Maybe they're still chasing her.

00:16:47.780 --> 00:16:51.080
What are you doing on my front porch, you little sneak?

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:54.790
Waiting for you, Lumpy.

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:57.320
Thought I told you to call me Clarence.

00:16:57.900 --> 00:16:59.160
All right, Clarence.

00:17:00.350 --> 00:17:01.270
What'd you come over for?

00:17:01.350 --> 00:17:02.750
To borrow my comic books again?

00:17:03.870 --> 00:17:05.530
Uh-uh. I wanted to talk to you.

00:17:06.060 --> 00:17:06.620
About what?

00:17:07.710 --> 00:17:11.320
Well, I want you to go to Coach Henderson

00:17:11.320 --> 00:17:13.840
and tell him you started all that stuff about the towel fight.

00:17:14.840 --> 00:17:17.540
Hey, Beeb, have you gone out of your skull or something?

00:17:18.340 --> 00:17:20.240
No. I just didn't get out of your skull.

00:17:20.260 --> 00:17:20.400
I didn't get out of your skull or something.

00:17:20.400 --> 00:17:20.740
That's all.

00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:24.700
Oh, sure. I ought to go throw myself over Niagara Falls, too.

00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:27.720
Don't you feel bad Wally got kicked off the team

00:17:27.720 --> 00:17:29.680
and can't go to Lineral with you guys?

00:17:30.320 --> 00:17:31.800
Sure, but that's the brakes.

00:17:32.180 --> 00:17:33.400
Anyway, why come to me?

00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:34.400
Why don't you go to Eddie?

00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:36.220
He started as much as I did.

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:39.310
Well, I figure you're both skunks,

00:17:40.600 --> 00:17:42.160
but he's a worse skunk than you are.

00:17:45.240 --> 00:17:46.880
Listen, I gotta go to Linwood.

00:17:47.180 --> 00:17:49.460
My pop and my whole family are driving up there,

00:17:49.830 --> 00:17:53.410
and if I disappoint my daddy, smack, right in the mouth.

00:17:54.830 --> 00:17:57.370
Yeah, what if you guys go up there,

00:17:57.850 --> 00:17:59.310
and then you lose the whole meet?

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:00.900
We might not lose it.

00:18:01.450 --> 00:18:02.590
Yeah, but you might.

00:18:03.420 --> 00:18:04.260
We might not.

00:18:04.890 --> 00:18:05.910
Yeah, but you might.

00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:09.370
Then what if after the meet, all the guys find out

00:18:09.370 --> 00:18:11.730
Mayfield got skunked on account of you and Eddie?

00:18:12.380 --> 00:18:14.020
What if they hear it was your fault,

00:18:14.120 --> 00:18:15.080
Wally, didn't get to go?

00:18:16.110 --> 00:18:17.910
They better not hear it, that's all.

00:18:18.760 --> 00:18:21.130
Yeah, but even if they don't know it,

00:18:21.290 --> 00:18:22.930
you'll know it for the rest of your life.

00:18:23.420 --> 00:18:24.120
So what?

00:18:24.920 --> 00:18:26.200
Well, it'll be inside your head,

00:18:26.320 --> 00:18:28.640
and it'll keep telling you what a big rat you are,

00:18:29.250 --> 00:18:29.970
and they're pretty s-

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:31.700
Soon you'll feel like even a bigger rat,

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:33.560
and all of a sudden you'll feel like

00:18:33.560 --> 00:18:35.060
the biggest rat in the whole world.

00:18:35.880 --> 00:18:37.280
Where do you get all this junk?

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:39.180
It happened in the movie once,

00:18:39.340 --> 00:18:40.860
to a guy I was mean to Jimmy Stewart.

00:18:42.220 --> 00:18:43.700
Beaver, you better beat it, see?

00:18:47.120 --> 00:18:52.810
Okay, but just remember what I said, lumpy.

00:18:53.270 --> 00:18:55.190
I told you, my real name is Clarence.

00:18:55.830 --> 00:18:59.980
I know it is, Clarence Wra-

00:19:02.330 --> 00:19:03.190
He's stupid as a rat.

00:19:12.570 --> 00:19:15.320
You mean you said all that to a big guy like Lumpy?

00:19:15.960 --> 00:19:16.960
Weren't you scared of him?

00:19:17.710 --> 00:19:18.810
Sure, I was just scared of him.

00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:22.560
Sometimes, if you don't let a big guy know you're scared of him,

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:24.460
he forgets he's such a big guy.

00:19:25.740 --> 00:19:26.100
Yeah.

00:19:29.540 --> 00:19:29.980
Hey, uh...

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:34.120
You didn't really think that Lumpy would tell the coach that I didn't start the fight, did you?

00:19:34.660 --> 00:19:39.440
Well, when I went over there, I figured maybe I could make an impression on his brain.

00:19:40.440 --> 00:19:44.750
But when he looked in the glass and called himself a doll, I figured it was kind of hopeless.

00:19:47.060 --> 00:19:48.420
Thanks for trying, Beeve.

00:19:49.970 --> 00:19:51.050
But let's face it, huh?

00:19:51.830 --> 00:19:54.490
I guess I don't have a chance in the world of getting to Linwood.

00:19:55.070 --> 00:19:59.760
Yeah, I saw Dad downstairs.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:01.140
Is he still mad at you?

00:20:02.190 --> 00:20:04.310
I don't know. I didn't want to ask him.

00:20:04.700 --> 00:20:06.280
Might start a whole big thing again.

00:20:06.840 --> 00:20:09.480
Yeah. Sometimes when a grown-up is mad at you,

00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:11.580
you can get in trouble just by saying hello.

00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:14.040
Yeah. Hello, Daddy.

00:20:14.920 --> 00:20:16.620
Well, what can I do for you, big champ?

00:20:17.520 --> 00:20:20.540
Well, I was thinking about the track meet up in Linwood.

00:20:20.930 --> 00:20:22.610
Yes, your mother and I are looking forward to it.

00:20:22.770 --> 00:20:24.550
I'm sure you're gonna come through for your old dad.

00:20:25.820 --> 00:20:27.760
Well, I don't think I'm gonna be going.

00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:29.960
What do you mean you're not gonna...

00:20:31.520 --> 00:20:34.500
Well, Wally got suspended off the team for throwing towels,

00:20:35.260 --> 00:20:36.920
but I was the one who really started it.

00:20:37.230 --> 00:20:39.500
You were? Yes, sir.

00:20:40.140 --> 00:20:42.920
And I'm gonna phone the coach tomorrow and tell him it was all my fault.

00:20:43.280 --> 00:20:45.370
You're gonna do what? Yes, sir.

00:20:45.660 --> 00:20:48.040
I thought it over in my head, and I think it's the right thing to do.

00:20:48.180 --> 00:20:50.220
Now, wait a minute. No use going off the deep end.

00:20:50.400 --> 00:20:52.500
Your mother and I have been planning this trip for a week.

00:20:53.380 --> 00:20:57.200
I know, but I think I ought to let the coach know as soon as I can.

00:20:58.280 --> 00:20:59.980
Would you phone him for me in case he might...

00:21:03.220 --> 00:21:05.820
Why'd you have to go and get yourself in messes like this?

00:21:06.480 --> 00:21:07.880
Gee, I don't know, Daddy.

00:21:08.120 --> 00:21:10.640
And stop calling me Daddy. You're too big for that.

00:21:11.690 --> 00:21:13.070
Yeah, but you always told me that I...

00:21:13.070 --> 00:21:14.810
Never mind what I always told you.

00:21:14.870 --> 00:21:17.030
Now, will you please get out of here? I've got some thinking to do.

00:21:17.620 --> 00:21:20.760
All right. I know how it upsets you whenever you have to do any thinking.

00:21:27.060 --> 00:21:29.310
Well, Mr. Rutherford called a few minutes ago.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:33.880
He talked to the coach, and Lumpy admitted that he started the towel fight.

00:21:34.970 --> 00:21:37.470
Well, that's certain he doesn't sound like Lumpy, does it?

00:21:38.320 --> 00:21:40.670
Gee, maybe Lumpy's tired of being a Lumpy.

00:21:41.920 --> 00:21:43.420
Gee, thanks a lot, Beeve.

00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:44.780
Thanks for what?

00:21:45.600 --> 00:21:46.920
Oh, uh, nothing, Dad.

00:21:48.030 --> 00:21:49.410
Boy, this is sure gonna be neat.

00:21:49.820 --> 00:21:52.420
Now I can go up to Linwood and watch Wally skunk the other team.

00:21:53.290 --> 00:21:56.330
We'll eat Mom's sandwiches and we'll potato salad and everything.

00:21:57.960 --> 00:21:59.260
Just a minute, Beaver.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:01.460
This doesn't take Wally off the hook.

00:22:01.700 --> 00:22:03.860
It just means that Eddie and Lumpy can't go either.

00:22:07.450 --> 00:22:10.420
But, well, gee, Dad, they started the whole thing

00:22:10.420 --> 00:22:11.660
about the towel fight and everything.

00:22:12.080 --> 00:22:14.400
Yes, but Wally threw towels too.

00:22:15.740 --> 00:22:17.220
Yeah, Dad, but it was self-defense.

00:22:18.110 --> 00:22:21.310
Well, like on television, if the other guy draws first,

00:22:21.690 --> 00:22:22.890
then he can blow his head off,

00:22:23.270 --> 00:22:24.470
and everybody pats you on the back

00:22:24.470 --> 00:22:25.750
and tells you what a neat guy you are.

00:22:26.590 --> 00:22:29.960
Well, Beaver, this may be-

00:22:30.030 --> 00:22:36.940
hard for you to believe but life isn't exactly like television that's right beaver wally shouldn't

00:22:36.940 --> 00:22:46.350
have thrown the towels at the other boys yeah i know mom but they made me so mad i i just couldn't

00:22:46.350 --> 00:22:52.510
help it well son you just got to learn not to let people make you that mad because what you do

00:22:52.510 --> 00:22:59.630
is your own responsibility no matter why you do it for instance if uh if someone cuts me off in traffic

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:01.900
That doesn't mean I have any right to cut them off.

00:23:02.620 --> 00:23:04.040
That's okay for goodness, Dad.

00:23:04.800 --> 00:23:06.640
But, boy, it's a tough rule for kids.

00:23:08.180 --> 00:23:09.380
I know it is, Beaver.

00:23:09.500 --> 00:23:11.730
But if you don't learn the rules when you're a kid,

00:23:11.770 --> 00:23:12.990
you may never learn them at all.

00:23:14.030 --> 00:23:15.070
We're sorry, Wally.

00:23:16.410 --> 00:23:17.470
Oh, that's okay.

00:23:18.400 --> 00:23:19.440
There'll be other track meets.

00:23:20.570 --> 00:23:21.010
Come on, Dave.

00:23:24.560 --> 00:23:29.970
Wally, go to some other...

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:31.780
where they didn't have any rules at all.

00:23:32.840 --> 00:23:35.240
Look, Beaver, I bet you even if you went to the moon,

00:23:35.280 --> 00:23:37.440
they'd have signs all over the place like, um,

00:23:38.110 --> 00:23:40.230
like, don't spit in the craters and junk like that.

00:23:40.690 --> 00:23:44.100
You know, I guess you can't have any real fun until you croak.

00:23:44.560 --> 00:23:58.370
You want to have to look at the boys? Uh-huh.

00:24:00.120 --> 00:24:11.270
well nicest looking boys i've ever seen must be asleep they are oh uh want some coffee

00:24:14.560 --> 00:24:21.040
ward could i ask you something sure when you were wally's age if someone hit you with a wet towel

00:24:21.040 --> 00:24:26.080
what would you have done you know i've been afraid somebody in this family would ask me

00:24:26.080 --> 00:24:29.900
that glad it was you well what would you have done

00:24:30.800 --> 00:24:33.640
Well, I'd probably have lost my temper and thrown the towel back.

00:24:34.260 --> 00:24:36.520
Why, Ward Cleaver, you're no better than your son.

00:24:37.170 --> 00:24:40.450
That's right. But what's wrong with wanting my son to be better than I was?

00:24:41.510 --> 00:24:43.850
Well, now that you mention it, nothing at all.

00:25:30.060 --> 00:25:30.520
Thank you.
